    bottomLine: "The compromise pick: enough weight to be acceptable downstairs, light enough to deliver, with the best screen at the price.",
    description: "The 1295i sits deliberately in the middle of every axis on this page, which in an apartment is often the right place to be. It is heavy enough that downstairs measurements are acceptable with a mat, light enough that delivery is not the ordeal the Sole F65 can be, and it folds without a struggle. The screen is the largest here outside the NordicTrack and costs considerably less. Incline to 12% is the specification we would point to for apartment use specifically: incline walking gives you most of running's cardiovascular benefit at close to walking's impact, which is exactly the trade a shared floor calls for. The reservation is the same as every iFit-ecosystem machine — the console is built to sell you a subscription and will keep reminding you. The hardware underneath is sound.",
  },
  {
    id: "nordictrack-c-990",
    name: "NordicTrack C 990",
    category: "Best Guided Content",
    badge: "Best Classes",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=NordicTrack%20C%20990+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: "/nordictrack-c-990-treadmill/",
    priceRange: "$999-$1,299",
    specs: "Assisted fold · iFit-ready · 60 in. deck · mid-weight frame",
    pros: [
      "Full 60-inch deck at a mid-range price",
      "Guided incline-walking content is well suited to apartment training",
      "Folds assisted and stands reasonably upright",
      "Better downstairs result than the lighter ProForm",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Close to unusable as intended without an iFit subscription",
      "Fold takes longer than the Sole machines and needs more effort",
    ],
    bottomLine: "Worth it only if you will actually use the guided content — the subscription is doing most of the work at this price.",
    description: "The C 990 is a reasonable apartment machine wrapped around a subscription proposition, and how you feel about that determines whether it belongs on your shortlist. The hardware is fine: a full 60-inch deck at a mid-range price, a mid-weight frame that measured better downstairs than the light ProForm, and an assisted fold that works even if it is slower and more effortful than the Sole machines' hydraulic assist. The guided incline-walking content is genuinely well matched to apartment use, where incline walking is the training approach that keeps you on good terms with your neighbours. But without the subscription the console is largely inert, and you are then paying mid-range money for a machine the F63 beats on every apartment-relevant measure. Buy it if you want the classes; otherwise buy the Sole.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Best Lists", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Treadmills for Apartments", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-treadmills-for-apartments/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will my downstairs neighbour hear my treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Some of it, yes, but less than you fear and not the part you expect. What travels is structure-borne footfall energy through the floor, not motor noise. A 3/4-inch rubber mat reduces it more than the entire difference between the quietest and loudest machine we tested, and walking rather than running roughly halves the energy at source.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are heavier treadmills louder in an apartment?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Beside the deck, often yes. In the room below, usually the opposite. Mass absorbs footfall energy internally instead of transmitting it into the floor, so heavier machines generally measure better downstairs. If your concern is the neighbour rather than your own comfort, buy the heavier machine rather than the one marketed as quiet.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a treadmill go up to a third-floor apartment?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Often, but it is the constraint to check first rather than last. Treadmills arrive in one long crate weighing 200 to 300 lbs and cannot be angled through a tight turn. Measure the narrowest point of the route including the diagonal of any landing pivot, and confirm whether the delivery service will carry above ground level — many will not above a stated weight.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does a treadmill mat actually make a difference?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It was the single most effective intervention in our whole test. Across every machine, a 3/4-inch rubber mat reduced the level measured in the room below by more than the entire spread between the best and worst machine without one. It also protects the floor and stops the frame walking. Treat it as part of the purchase, not an accessory.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is my apartment floor strong enough for a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Almost certainly. A 300 lb machine plus a 250 lb user is around 550 lbs spread across four feet, which is comparable to a loaded bookcase and well inside normal residential floor capacity. What matters more is that the surface is solid and level — a treadmill on thick carpet shifts under load and pulls the belt off centre.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I buy a walking pad instead?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "If your use is walking rather than running, probably yes. Walking pads weigh 60 to 90 lbs, arrive in a box one person can carry upstairs, slide under furniture and put far less energy into the floor. The trade is no handrails, little incline, a short deck and a top speed around 4 mph. For walking while working they are the better apartment answer.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-08",
      dateModified: "2026-09-08",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      offers: {
        "@type": "AggregateOffer",
        priceCurrency: "USD",
        availability: "https://schema.org/InStock",
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

