  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-09-08",
    modifiedTime: "2026-09-08",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "treadmills for apartments",
      "apartment treadmill",
      "quiet treadmill",
      "small space treadmill",
      "folding treadmill",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See the Top Pick", link: "#sole-f63" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-besttreadmillsforapartments" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-besttreadmillsforapartments)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Treadmills for <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Apartments</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Four constraints decide this, and only one of them is noise. Footprint when folded, the stairwell the machine has to arrive through, and what your lease actually says matter just as much.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#sole-f63" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            See the Top Pick
          </a>
          <a href="#constraints" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            The Four Constraints
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">September 8, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">Two guides, different jobs.</strong> This page is about
          the whole apartment constraint set — noise, footprint, delivery and your lease. If
          your only question is which machines are quietest and why, our
          <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmill guide</a>
          covers the acoustics in far more detail, including measurements taken in the room
          below.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#constraints" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. The Four Constraints, In the Order They Bite</a>
          <a href="#noise-truth" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. What the Neighbour Below Actually Hears</a>
          <a href="#how-we-tested" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. How We Tested for Apartment Use</a>
          <a href="#picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. The Six Best Treadmills for Apartments</a>
          <a href="#walking-pads" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. When a Walking Pad Is the Better Answer</a>
          <a href="#lease" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Leases, Hours and Neighbour Management</a>
          <a href="#setup" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Setting It Up in a Small Room</a>
          <a href="#moving" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Moving Out, Storage and Resale</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Final Recommendations</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Buying a treadmill for an apartment is not mainly a noise problem, though that is the
          part everyone worries about. It is four problems at once, and the one that ends most
          purchases is the stairwell — a machine that fits the room perfectly and cannot get
          into it is still the wrong machine.
        </p>

        <div id="constraints" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Four Constraints, In the Order They Bite</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The stairwell and the door.</strong> This is the constraint that most often
            ends a purchase, and it is the one people check last. Treadmills arrive in a single
            crate, often 6 feet long and 200 to 300 lbs, and unlike flat-packed furniture they
            cannot be angled through a tight turn. A third-floor walk-up with a half-landing
            pivot rules out most of the market regardless of anything else. Measure the narrowest
            point of the route, including the diagonal of any turn, before you look at a single
            product page.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The neighbour below.</strong> Treadmill noise splits into two kinds and they
            behave completely differently. Airborne noise — the motor, the belt, the fan — is
            what you hear standing beside the machine and it is largely contained by walls.
            Structure-borne noise is footfall energy travelling through the frame into the floor
            slab, and it is what your downstairs neighbour hears. Heavier machines are usually
            louder beside the deck and quieter downstairs, because mass absorbs impact rather
            than transmitting it. That inversion catches people out constantly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Floor space, folded and unfolded.</strong> An apartment treadmill usually has
            to disappear between sessions. What matters is the folded footprint and the fold
            mechanism, not the advertised "space-saving" label. A machine that technically folds
            but needs two people and four minutes to do it will simply be left up, at which point
            its unfolded dimensions are the real ones.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The lease.</strong> Many leases contain clauses about exercise equipment,
            floor loading or noise hours, and some buildings prohibit treadmills outright. This
            costs nothing to check and occasionally saves a great deal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise-truth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What the Neighbour Below Actually Hears</h2>
<p>
            The single most useful thing to understand here is that the sound you hear and the
            sound they hear are produced by different mechanisms and rank machines differently.
            When we measured beside the deck and in the room below the same machines, the two
            rankings did not match — the quietest machine at the console was mid-table
            downstairs, and one of the louder machines beside the deck was among the best below.
          </p>
          <p>
            The reason is mass and isolation. A heavy frame on good deck isolation absorbs
            footfall energy internally; a light frame passes it into the floor. Motor noise,
            which is what dominates the sound beside the machine, barely travels through a
            concrete or joisted floor at all. So if your concern is the neighbour rather than
            your own comfort, buy the heavier machine, not the one marketed as quiet.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three interventions genuinely help, in descending order of effect. A proper rubber
            treadmill mat, at least 3/4 inch thick, decouples the frame from the floor and is by
            some distance the most effective thing you can buy. Walking rather than running
            roughly halves the impact energy at source. And placement matters — over a
            load-bearing wall rather than mid-span, and never in a corner where the frame can
            couple into two walls. Our
            <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmill guide</a>
            has the measurements behind all three.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Tested for Apartment Use</h2>
<p>
            Every machine here completed our standard protocol first. For this guide we added
            four apartment-specific assessments. We measured the crate dimensions and assembled
            weight against a typical apartment delivery route, including a 32-inch doorway and a
            half-landing turn. We timed the fold and unfold with one person, because that is the
            realistic case in an apartment. We measured folded footprint rather than trusting the
            marketing figure. And we recorded sound pressure both beside the deck and in the room
            directly below, at walking and jogging speeds, with and without a mat.
          </p>
          <p>
            The mat result was the most decisive finding in the whole test. Across every machine,
            adding a 3/4-inch rubber mat reduced the level measured downstairs by more than the
            entire spread between the best and worst machine without one. Put plainly: the mat
            matters more than the machine. If you take one thing from this page, take that.
          </p>
          <p>
            The fold-time result was the second. Two machines that fold on paper took over two
            minutes and real effort with one person, and in an apartment that is the difference
            between a machine that gets put away and a machine that becomes furniture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Six Best Treadmills for Apartments</h2>
<p>
            Ranked on the four constraints together. Folded dimensions are our own measurements;
            fold times are one-person averages over five attempts.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Product cards -->
      <div class="not-prose">
        {
          PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between flex-wrap gap-3">
                  <div>
                    <p class="text-white font-black text-xl leading-tight">
                      {index + 1}. {product.name}
                    </p>
                    <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs">{product.category}</p>
                  </div>
                  <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
                    {product.badge}
                  </span>
                </div>
                <div class="p-6">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-4 flex-wrap">
                    <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-400">Typical street price</span>
                    <span class="ml-auto font-black text-gray-900">{product.priceRange}</span>
                  </div>
                  <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-400 mb-4">{product.specs}</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6" set:html={product.description} />
                  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-green-50 border border-green-100 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-green-700 mb-2">What Works</p>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.pros.map((pro) => (
                          <li class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">{pro}</li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">What Doesn't</p>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.cons.map((con) => (
                          <li class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">{con}</li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="border-t border-gray-100 pt-4">
                    <p class="text-gray-900 font-bold mb-4">{product.bottomLine}</p>
                    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
                      <a href={product.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
                        Check Price on Amazon &rarr;
                      </a>
                      {product.reviewUrl && (
                        <a href={product.reviewUrl} class="text-[#0F62FE] font-bold no-underline text-sm hover:underline">
                          Read our full {product.name} review &rarr;
                        </a>
                      )}
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="walking-pads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. When a Walking Pad Is the Better Answer</h2>
<p>
            For a meaningful share of apartment buyers, the right machine is not a treadmill at
            all. A walking pad — a low, deck-only machine with no upright or handrails — solves
            the apartment problem almost completely: it arrives in a box one person can carry up
            stairs, it slides under a bed or sofa, it weighs 60 to 90 lbs rather than 250, and it
            puts far less energy into the floor.
          </p>
          <p>
            The trade is real and worth stating plainly. Walking pads top out around 4 mph, have
            short decks unsuited to running, offer little or no incline, and have no handrails,
            which makes them a poor choice for anyone who wants support. If your use is walking
            while working or walking while watching something, they are excellent and the
            constraints above stop mattering.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk treadmill guide</a>
            covers the category properly, and
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad versus treadmill</a>
            works through the decision directly. If you are certain you want to run, skip both and
            stay on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lease" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Leases, Hours and Neighbour Management</h2>
<p>
            Read the lease before ordering. Clauses covering exercise equipment, floor loading or
            quiet hours are common, and a small number of buildings prohibit treadmills outright,
            usually in older properties with timber floors. It is a five-minute check that
            occasionally saves an expensive return.
          </p>
          <p>
            Beyond the paperwork, the practical advice is straightforward. Keep sessions inside
            ordinary waking hours — a 6 a.m. run above someone's bedroom generates complaints
            that the same run at 6 p.m. would not. Walking rather than running roughly halves the
            impact energy, and incline walking gives you most of the cardiovascular benefit of
            running at close to walking's impact, which makes it the ideal apartment training
            approach.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you share a floor with someone, telling them before the machine arrives is
            disproportionately effective. A neighbour who has been asked is far more tolerant
            than one who is surprised, and they will tell you what they actually hear, which is
            information you cannot otherwise get. Ask them to listen while you run the machine at
            a few speeds, and adjust placement based on what they report rather than what you
            assume.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Setting It Up in a Small Room</h2>
<p>
            Clearance behind the deck is a safety requirement rather than a nicety: it is where
            you go if you lose your footing, and in a small room the temptation to back the
            machine against a wall is strong. Leave as much run-off as the room allows and never
            place a wall, radiator or furniture directly behind the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ceiling height deserves a check that most people skip. The deck sits 6 to 10 inches
            off the floor before any incline is applied, and raising the front adds several
            inches more. Add your height to the deck height, add a few inches for stride, and
            compare against the ceiling — apartments with 8-foot ceilings and a tall user can run
            out of headroom on incline. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions guide</a>
            has the full clearance arithmetic.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, avoid putting the machine on carpet if you can. Carpet fibres are drawn into
            the motor housing, the pile blocks the ventilation the motor relies on, and a soft
            base lets the frame shift under load which pulls the belt off centre. If carpet is
            unavoidable, a rigid mat between the two solves most of it —
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our guide to treadmills on carpet</a>
            covers the specifics.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moving" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Moving Out, Storage and Resale</h2>
<p>
            Apartment tenancies are shorter than treadmill lifespans, and the machine you buy
            will probably have to come apart, go down the stairs and go into another building
            before it wears out. That is worth factoring into the purchase in a way it never is
            for someone buying for a house.
            </p>
          <p>
            Two properties matter. The first is whether the machine can be partially
            disassembled — some fold flat enough at the upright to shed several inches of
            crate depth, which can be the difference between fitting a lift and not. The second
            is weight, again: the machine that was difficult to get in will be difficult to get
            out, and you will be doing it on a moving day rather than with a delivery crew.
          </p>
          <p>
            Resale is the other half of the arithmetic and it is more favourable than most
            people expect. Treadmills hold value poorly on paper and sell readily in practice,
            because the buyer is usually collecting locally and avoiding a delivery problem of
            their own. Machines from brands with no subscription requirement — Sole in
            particular — sell noticeably faster second-hand, because the buyer is purchasing
            hardware rather than inheriting an account. If you expect to move within a few
            years, that is a real argument for a subscription-free machine. Our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            covers what a buyer will inspect, which is also a useful list of what to keep in
            good order while you own it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Final Recommendations</h2>
<p>
            For most apartment buyers the <strong>Sole F63</strong> is the machine: heavy enough
            to keep footfall out of the floor below, a straightforward one-person hydraulic fold,
            and no subscription. It is not the smallest thing here, and if it fits your stairwell
            it is the one to buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the stairwell is the binding constraint, the <strong>ProForm Power 995i</strong>
            is the lightest real treadmill here and the only one on this list a determined pair
            can carry up two flights. If your use is walking rather than running, stop reading
            about treadmills and buy a walking pad — the
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk guide</a>
            covers those, and for an apartment they are a genuinely better answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever you buy, buy the mat with it. It reduced downstairs noise more than the
            entire difference between the best and worst machine in our test, and it costs a
            fraction of any of them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will my downstairs neighbour hear my treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some of it, yes, but less than you fear and not the part you expect. What travels is structure-borne footfall energy through the floor, not motor noise. A 3/4-inch rubber mat reduces it more than the entire difference between the quietest and loudest machine we tested, and walking rather than running roughly halves the energy at source.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are heavier treadmills louder in an apartment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Beside the deck, often yes. In the room below, usually the opposite. Mass absorbs footfall energy internally instead of transmitting it into the floor, so heavier machines generally measure better downstairs. If your concern is the neighbour rather than your own comfort, buy the heavier machine rather than the one marketed as quiet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a treadmill go up to a third-floor apartment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, but it is the constraint to check first rather than last. Treadmills arrive in one long crate weighing 200 to 300 lbs and cannot be angled through a tight turn. Measure the narrowest point of the route including the diagonal of any landing pivot, and confirm whether the delivery service will carry above ground level — many will not above a stated weight.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a treadmill mat actually make a difference?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It was the single most effective intervention in our whole test. Across every machine, a 3/4-inch rubber mat reduced the level measured in the room below by more than the entire spread between the best and worst machine without one. It also protects the floor and stops the frame walking. Treat it as part of the purchase, not an accessory.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is my apartment floor strong enough for a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost certainly. A 300 lb machine plus a 250 lb user is around 550 lbs spread across four feet, which is comparable to a loaded bookcase and well inside normal residential floor capacity. What matters more is that the surface is solid and level — a treadmill on thick carpet shifts under load and pulls the belt off centre.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a walking pad instead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If your use is walking rather than running, probably yes. Walking pads weigh 60 to 90 lbs, arrive in a box one person can carry upstairs, slide under furniture and put far less energy into the floor. The trade is no handrails, little incline, a short deck and a top speed around 4 mph. For walking while working they are the better apartment answer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Treadmill for an Apartment</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first of these ends more purchases than the other three combined, and it is the one people check last.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring the room but not the route</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill arrives as one 6-foot crate weighing 200 to 300 lbs and cannot be angled around a tight landing the way flat-packed furniture can. Measure the narrowest point of the whole route, including the diagonal of any turn, and confirm the delivery service will carry above ground level. Do this before you shortlist anything.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the machine marketed as quiet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Marketing measures noise beside the deck, which is airborne motor and belt noise and is not what travels through a floor. Your neighbour hears structure-borne footfall energy, and heavier machines generally do better on that despite being louder at the console. The two rankings did not match in our testing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the mat as optional</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 3/4-inch rubber mat cut our downstairs measurements by more than the entire difference between the best and worst machine in the test. It is the cheapest and most effective thing you can do about apartment noise, and it also protects the floor and stops the frame walking under load. Buy it at the same time as the treadmill.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not checking the lease</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Clauses covering exercise equipment, floor loading and quiet hours are common, and a small number of buildings — usually older ones with timber floors — prohibit treadmills outright. It is a five-minute read that occasionally saves an expensive return and an awkward conversation with a building manager.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
